# Criminal Law Archive

This project is a dignified digital archive for a retired criminal law professor.

Korean-language essays and series. Prefer minimal, focused diffs that match existing patterns.

## Project Identity

This is NOT:

- a startup website
- a SaaS landing page
- a marketing site
- a news media outlet

This IS:

- a scholarly archive
- a legal essay collection
- a long-term knowledge repository
- a digital legacy project

## Design Principles

Use:

- generous whitespace
- calm typography
- restrained visual hierarchy
- timeless layouts
- academic tone

Avoid:

- flashy gradients
- aggressive call-to-actions
- startup aesthetics
- social media style engagement patterns

## Typography

Headings:

- Noto Serif KR

Body:

- Noto Sans KR

Typography should feel:

- authoritative
- thoughtful
- readable
- timeless

Implementation: long-form MDX uses `.archive-prose`; Korean copy uses `.text-keep`. Page rhythm: small uppercase eyebrow → serif `h1` → muted lead → `border-line` sections.

## Technical Stack

- Next.js App Router
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- Vercel

Future:

- Supabase CMS

### Next.js (16.x)

- Read `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before changing framework code; APIs differ from older Next.js.
- `app/` pages are async Server Components. Dynamic `params` are `Promise<{ slug: string }>`.
- No `middleware`, `app/api`, or route groups yet. Use `generateStaticParams` on `[slug]` routes where content is static.
- Path alias: `@/*` → project root.

### Content

- Essays live in `content/essays/*.mdx` (exclude `_*.mdx` from publishing).
- All reads/writes go through `lib/essays.ts` — do not duplicate filesystem logic in pages.
- Frontmatter: `title`, `description`, `date`, `category`, `series`, `draft`, `featured` (strict string/boolean validation).
- Series are derived from the `series` field, not separate files. Slugs use `getSeriesSlug()` (NFKC + Korean-safe).
- Rendering: `gray-matter` + `next-mdx-remote/rsc` on `app/essays/[slug]/page.tsx`. Draft essays must `notFound()` and stay out of public lists.

### UI components

- Tailwind v4 tokens in `app/globals.css`: `paper`, `ink`, `line`, `accent`, `max-w-reading`, `max-w-wide`.
- Reuse `Section`, `Container`, `ArticleCard`, `SiteHeader`, `SiteFooter`.

## CMS Philosophy

The primary user is a retired criminal law professor.

Prioritize:

- simplicity
- reliability
- clarity

Avoid:

- complex dashboards
- unnecessary settings
- feature overload

When adding Supabase: keep public URLs `/essays/[slug]` and `/series/[slug]`; add `lib/content/` (or adapter behind `lib/essays.ts`), `app/admin/`, auth middleware, and revalidation. Store stable series slugs if titles can change.

## Development Rules

Before making large changes:

1. Explain the plan.
2. List affected files.
3. Make incremental changes.
4. Preserve existing functionality.

## Visual Reference

The desired quality level is similar to premium editorial journals.

References:

- The New Yorker
- Aeon
- Long Now
- Second Season Project

The site should feel closer to a journal than a blog.

## Commands

```bash
npm run dev    # local
npm run lint && npm run build   # before deploy
```

New essays: copy `content/essays/_template.mdx`, set `draft: false` to publish.
